# Protected regions

CleanStack owns the files it generates. Hand-written code survives
regeneration only inside protected regions. AI agents must internalize these
rules before touching any generated file.

## Marker format

A region is a comment pair whose comment syntax matches the file language:

```text
Java, TypeScript, SCSS  // <cleanstack:user-code id="service.product">  ...  // </cleanstack:user-code>
HTML, Markdown, YAML    <!-- <cleanstack:user-code id="...">  ...  -->
SQL                     -- <cleanstack:user-code id="...">     ...  --
```

The `id` is stable and namespaced (for example `controller.product`); a
checksum detects whether the generated side of the region changed.

## Rules for agents

1. **Never move, rename, or reformat marker lines.** The opening and closing
   lines must stay byte-identical.
2. **Write custom code only inside regions.** Anything written outside a region
   in a generated file is overwritten by the next `cleanstack regenerate`.
3. **Never delete a region**, even when it looks empty; the generator re-injects
   its content on regeneration.
4. **New files you create yourself** (a new use case, a shared component) are
   yours; the generator never touches files it did not generate. Follow
   [naming-conventions.md](naming-conventions.md) so they fit in.
5. **Do not edit `.cleanstack/project.json`.** It stores generation metadata for
   `regenerate`; hand edits corrupt the next run.

## Regeneration workflow

```bash
cleanstack regenerate --dry-run   # preview what would change
cleanstack regenerate             # apply, keeping every protected region
```

Outcomes reported per file: `created`, `updated` (regions re-injected),
`unchanged`, `preserved` (file has no regions; never overwritten without
`--force`), `conflict` (a region was edited on both sides).

- A `conflict` blocks the run. Fix the region by hand, or rerun with `--force`
  to discard your changes deliberately.
- After structural template updates, run `cleanstack regenerate --all` and
  review the report before committing.

